> ===
> We focus on reporting results in 2050, under a range of “business-as-usual” scenarios found in the supporting literature. Where possible, we translate results according to a baseline future climate scenario which combines the baseline, high-emissions climate projections from the IPCC with estimates of the effects of tipping points from Dietz et al. (2021) (see appendix A.1).
> 
> Under a high-emissions baseline scenario, the world is projected to warm globally by 2.5 °C [2.2 – 2.8 °C] above pre-industrial levels by 2050 and 3.9 °C [3.5 – 4.3 °C] by 2100. This high emissions scenario results in much higher damages than the low emissions SSP1-2.6 scenario, which has 2 °C [1.8 – 2.3 °C] warming by 2050. These warming levels are higher than the results from the IPCC for the same scenarios because of the effect of tipping points missing from the original analyses.
> Unless emissions are rapidly reduced, the next 25 years are projected to produce 50% as much additional warming, for the average person, as the past 250 years. The average person lives in a country that is about 2 °C warmer in 2025 than it was under pre-industrial conditions, and this could increase to about 3 °C. This additional warming will come with a variety of regional and global tipping points exacerbating the climate problem. Across the globe, such rapid warming will drive massive heat-related mortality, wide-spread labour productivity loss, crop failures, flooding, droughts, ecosystem disruption, and many other direct impacts. How countries respond to these impacts could amplify or avoid their worst consequences.
> 
> As the global climate crisis intensifies, the need for investment in adaptation strategies has become more pressing than ever. Severe losses lie ahead; without stronger action, some nations in sub-Saharan Africa and Southeast Asia could face GDP losses in 2050 exceeding 20% due to temperature shocks alone, while total welfare losses are expected to be far higher. Alarmingly, many least developed countries are already experiencing the negative impacts of climate change, with estimates suggesting they could be 10% poorer today as a result of current warming.
> 
> Labour productivity loss is projected to exceed 5% in regions like Africa and Asia, while TFP growth could be reduced by up to 0.3 percentage points per year, suggesting potential long-term economic stagnation. These macroeconomic changes are projected to have fiscal consequences that grow through mid-century. Climate change is expected to threaten sovereign credit ratings across the globe, especially in high-income countries. 
> 
> However, there is a ray of hope in the form of smart investment in adaptation. Adaptation actions deliver a median economic benefit-cost ratio of at least 4 to 1, and the median internal rate of return is an impressive 25%. This makes adaptation not just a necessary response but one of the best economic investments available, capable of halving the projected impacts of climate change on economies.
> 
> Proactive action on the part of governments, including MoF, can both reduce these risks and pave the way to concessionary funding. As a national average, adaptation financing that supports locally-targeted adaptation can reduce risks by 38 - 72% (World Bank 2024). The rise in borrowing costs can be reduced or reversed through investment in resilience with every 1% change in resilience reducing the change in borrowing costs by 0.75% in developing countries (IMF 2020).
> 
> Overall, these findings underscore the critical role of proactive adaptation strategies in mitigating both immediate and long-term economic and welfare losses, highlighting the importance of investing in resilience to safeguard vulnerable economies against the escalating risks of climate change.
